(a) No hazardous waste shall be transported across, within or through the county, except:
(1) If it is accompanied by a manifest properly issued, completed and filled out in accordance with the rules and regulations of the board of natural resources of the state.
(2) If a copy of the manifest and the proposed route of travel across, within or through the county are filed with the sheriff of the county at least three full working days prior to any transport.
(3) That the vehicle transporting said hazardous waste shall travel only state routes or those roads clearly marked and posted as permitting such transport unless express written permission is granted by the board of commissioners and the sheriff for use of other county road or roads for transport; provided, however, that no subdivision street shall be used for such transport.
(b) Any vehicle transporting hazardous waste across, within or through the county, in addition to any and all other requirements herein, shall:
(1) Be accompanied by police escort, either the county sheriff's department or an escort approved by the sheriff. Any escort must be approved by the sheriff as to the route of travel, number of vehicles necessary for escort, time of travel and any other provisions deemed reasonable and necessary by the sheriff, if different from those contained herein. The owner or operator of said vehicle shall pay for said escort through the county, regardless of the agency providing the same, at a minimum rate of $50.00 for each escort vehicle (minimum of two);
(2) Maintain a distance of at least 150 feet from the nearest vehicle, except escort vehicles;
(3) Drive at a rate of speed no greater than 30 miles per hour on any road in the unincorporated county; provided, however, that the sheriff shall have authority to require a lower speed limit on any county road where such transport has been authorized by the board of commissioners;
(4) Confine all transport of hazardous waste so that such transport shall occur between the hours of 10:00 p.m. and 1:30 a.m.;
(5) Restrict transport of hazardous waste so that no such transport shall occur during times of inclement weather or when inclement weather is forecast and is imminent within six hours. As used herein, the term "inclement weather" shall include snow, ice, sleet, heavy rain of one inch or more per hour, tornadoes, extreme lightning, high winds in excess of 25 miles per hour or any combination thereof.
(c) Prior to any transport of hazardous waste across, within or through the county, the owner or operator of the vehicle transporting said hazardous waste shall file a copy of the route across, within or through the county with the sheriff.
(Ord. of 4-2-1991, § 4)

191:
It is the purpose of this chapter to promote the public health, safety and general welfare and to minimize public and private losses due to flood conditions in specific areas by provisions designed to:
(1) Require that uses vulnerable to floods, including facilities, which serve such uses, be protected against flood damage at the time of initial construction;
(2) Restrict or prohibit uses which are dangerous to health, safety and property due to water or erosion hazards, or which increase flood heights, velocities, or erosion;
(3) Control filling, grading, dredging and other development which may increase flood damage or erosion; and;
(4) Prevent or regulate the construction of flood barriers which will unnaturally divert flood waters or which may increase flood hazards to other lands;
(5) Control the alteration of natural floodplains, stream channels, and natural protective barriers, which are involved in the accommodation of floodwaters.
(Ord. Of 11-4-2019, (Art. 1, § C))

192:
The objectives of this chapter are:
(1) To protect human life and health;
(2) To minimize damage to public facilities and utilities such as water and gas mains, electric, telephone and sewer lines, streets and bridges located in floodplains;
(3) To help maintain a stable tax base by providing for the sound use and development of flood prone areas in such a manner as to minimize flood blight areas,
(4) To minimize expenditure of public money for costly flood control projects;
(5) To minimize the need for rescue and relief efforts associated with flooding and generally undertaken at the expense of the general public;
(6) To minimize prolonged business interruptions, and;
(7) To insure that potential homebuyers are notified that property is in a flood area.
(Ord. Of 11-4-2019, (Art. 1, § A))

